Privileges control access to data and restrict the actions users can perform. Two types:

1. System privileges — the right to perform a specific action at database
level on any schema, e.g. CREATE SESSION, CREATE TABLE, CREATE USER, ALTER SYSTEM, UNLIMITED TABLESPACE. Powerful ones go only to trusted users.

GRANT CREATE SESSION, CREATE TABLE TO john;
GRANT CREATE ANY TABLE TO john WITH ADMIN OPTION;  -- john can re-grant
REVOKE CREATE TABLE FROM john;

2. Object privileges — granted on a specific schema object owned by a
user: SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, EXECUTE, REFERENCES, ALTER, INDEX, READ, etc.

GRANT SELECT, UPDATE ON customer TO james;
GRANT SELECT ON customer TO james WITH GRANT OPTION; -- james can re-grant
GRANT INSERT (customer_id) ON customer TO james;     -- column privilege
REVOKE UPDATE ON customer FROM james;

Privileges can be granted directly to a user, or to a role which is then granted to users; PUBLIC grants to every user. With WITH GRANT OPTION the grantee may pass an object privilege on; when a grantor loses (the revoke cascades) the privileges granted by that grantee are also removed. System privileges use WITH ADMIN OPTION (not cascading on revoke).

Privilege = a right to execute a particular SQL statement or to access another user's object. It is required because tables/schemas are private and security demands least privilege: without explicit privileges no user can log in (CREATE SESSION), create objects or read other schemas' data.

System privileges (database-level right to perform an action anywhere): CREATE SESSION (log in), CREATE TABLE / CREATE ANY TABLE (own/any schema), CREATE USER, CREATE VIEW, CREATE SEQUENCE, CREATE TABLESPACE, ALTER SYSTEM, UNLIMITED TABLESPACE (very powerful), DROP ANY TABLE, EXECUTE ANY PROCEDURE. They are granted with optional WITH ADMIN OPTION so the grantee can re-grant.

Object privileges (on a specific object of another user): SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE (data), EXECUTE (programs), REFERENCES (foreign keys), ALTER, INDEX, READ (directories) — granted with optional WITH GRANT OPTION.

Commands:

-- a) Create common user and local user (multitenant)
CREATE USER C##admin IDENTIFIED BY pwd123;      -- common (CDB root)
CREATE USER local_user IDENTIFIED BY L0cal!pwd; -- local (inside a PDB)

-- b) Create a role, assign two system privileges, assign to user
CREATE ROLE reporting;
GRANT CREATE SESSION, CREATE VIEW TO reporting;
GRANT reporting TO alice;

-- c) Unlimited space on 'users' tablespace
ALTER USER alice QUOTA UNLIMITED ON users;

-- d) Create profile with the given parameters
CREATE PROFILE dev_profile LIMIT
  FAILED_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS 2
  IDLE_TIME 5
  SESSIONS_PER_USER 3;

-- e) Assign profile to the user
ALTER USER alice PROFILE dev_profile;

A profile is a named set of resource limits and password-management rules; every user is assigned exactly one profile (DEFAULT if not specified).

-- create the profile
CREATE PROFILE dev_profile LIMIT
  SESSIONS_PER_USER   3        -- concurrent sessions
  IDLE_TIME           5        -- minutes idle before disconnect
  FAILED_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS 2      -- lock account after 2 bad logins
  PASSWORD_LIFE_TIME  60
  PASSWORD_LOCK_TIME  1/24;

-- assign to an existing user
ALTER USER alice PROFILE dev_profile;

-- or assign at creation
CREATE USER bob IDENTIFIED BY pwd123 PROFILE dev_profile;

Verify with SELECT * FROM dba_profiles WHERE profile='DEV_PROFILE'; and SELECT username, profile FROM dba_users;

RMAN (Recovery Manager) needs a target database and, at minimum, uses the control file as its repository:

1. Set the environment — ORACLE_HOME, ORACLE_SID and connect as a
user with SYSDBA privilege.
2. Configure the fast recovery area (recommended disk location for
backups):

ALTER SYSTEM SET DB_RECOVERY_FILE_DEST_SIZE = 10G;
ALTER SYSTEM SET DB_RECOVERY_FILE_DEST = '/u01/fast_recovery_area';

3. Put the database in ARCHIVELOG mode so online (hot) backups are
recoverable: SHUTDOWN IMMEDIATE; STARTUP MOUNT; ALTER DATABASE ARCHIVELOG; ALTER DATABASE OPEN; and set archivelog destination with LOG_ARCHIVE_DEST_1.
4. Start RMAN and connect to the target:

$ rman
RMAN> CONNECT TARGET /
connected to target database: PROD (DBID=39525561)

5. Configure persistent RMAN settings (stored in the control-file
repository):

CONFIGURE RETENTION POLICY TO RECOVERY WINDOW OF 7 DAYS;
CONFIGURE DEFAULT DEVICE TYPE TO DISK;
CONFIGURE CONTROLFILE AUTOBACKUP ON;
CONFIGURE BACKUP OPTIMIZATION ON;

6. Run backups — BACKUP DATABASE;,
BACKUP DATABASE PLUS ARCHIVELOG;, BACKUP INCREMENTAL LEVEL 0 DATABASE; and verify with LIST BACKUP; / RESTORE DATABASE VALIDATE;

Optionally configure a media manager for tape (SBT) and a recovery catalog schema for long-term metadata.

i. Create the table named tblbank having fields (Acc-no, name, address, ph-no, Balance)
ii. Add primary key in above table iii. Add check constraints in the balance field, value of balance > 300 iv. Rename the table tblbank to Bank
v. Insert some rows and delete one of the record from the table Bank
vi. Create the logical backup of above table using expdp utility vii. Give select, update privilege to another user Mohan viii. Add loan_id field as foreign key referencing the table loan ix. Modify the name field to increase the character length
x. Rename the name field to cname

-- (i) create the table
CREATE TABLE tblbank (
  acc_no   NUMBER(10),
  name     VARCHAR2(50),
  address  VARCHAR2(100),
  ph_no    VARCHAR2(15),
  balance  NUMBER(12,2)
);

-- (ii) add primary key
ALTER TABLE tblbank ADD CONSTRAINT pk_bank PRIMARY KEY (acc_no);

-- (iii) check constraint on balance
ALTER TABLE tblbank ADD CONSTRAINT chk_balance CHECK (balance > 300);

-- (iv) rename the table
RENAME tblbank TO Bank;   -- or: ALTER TABLE tblbank RENAME TO Bank;

-- (v) insert rows and delete one record
INSERT INTO Bank VALUES (101,'Ram Sharma','Kathmandu','9841000000', 50000);
INSERT INTO Bank VALUES (102,'Sita Karki','Pokhara','9846000000', 75000);
COMMIT;
DELETE FROM Bank WHERE acc_no = 101;
COMMIT;

-- (vi) logical backup using Data Pump export
-- Operating system:
expdp system/manager DIRECTORY=dpump_dir DUMPFILE=bank.dmp
  TABLES=Bank

-- (vii) privileges to user Mohan
GRANT SELECT, UPDATE ON Bank TO Mohan;

-- (viii) foreign key to table loan
ALTER TABLE Bank ADD (loan_id NUMBER(10));
ALTER TABLE Bank ADD CONSTRAINT fk_loan
  FOREIGN KEY (loan_id) REFERENCES loan(loan_id);

-- (ix) increase length of name
ALTER TABLE Bank MODIFY (name VARCHAR2(100));

-- (x) rename the column
ALTER TABLE Bank RENAME COLUMN name TO cname;

-- i. create the table emp
CREATE TABLE emp (
  emp_id  NUMBER(6),
  name    VARCHAR2(50),
  address VARCHAR2(100),
  dob     DATE,
  salary  NUMBER(10,2)
);

-- ii. add primary key
ALTER TABLE emp ADD CONSTRAINT pk_emp PRIMARY KEY (emp_id);

-- iii. check constraint on salary
ALTER TABLE emp ADD CONSTRAINT chk_salary CHECK (salary > 10000);

-- iv. rename the table emp to employee
RENAME emp TO employee;   -- ALTER TABLE emp RENAME TO employee;

-- v. insert rows and delete one record
INSERT INTO employee VALUES (1,'Rita Rai','Lalitpur',TO_DATE('1998-05-12','YYYY-MM-DD'),25000);
INSERT INTO employee VALUES (2,'Hari Thapa','Bhaktapur',TO_DATE('1995-09-30','YYYY-MM-DD'),30000);
COMMIT;
DELETE FROM employee WHERE emp_id = 1;
COMMIT;

-- vi. logical backup using expdp (OS command)
expdp system/manager DIRECTORY=dpump DUMPFILE=emp.dmp TABLES=employee

-- vii. privileges to another user User1
GRANT SELECT, UPDATE ON employee TO User1;

-- viii. foreign key department_id -> department
ALTER TABLE employee ADD (department_id NUMBER(6));
ALTER TABLE employee ADD CONSTRAINT fk_dept
  FOREIGN KEY (department_id) REFERENCES department(department_id);

-- ix. increase name length
ALTER TABLE employee MODIFY (name VARCHAR2(100));

-- x. rename name column to ename
ALTER TABLE employee RENAME COLUMN name TO ename;
